Root cause chain, each step captured via qm guest exec unless noted:

1. gpresult /r /scope:computer showed 5 applied GPOs, two never before captured in this project:
   District Lockdown, Secure Admin WS (verified-claims.md:19 only recorded 2 — was incomplete,
   not wrong, see report for the correction note).

2. Get-GPOReport -Name "Secure Admin WS" -ReportType Xml, grepped for Logon/Deny/Interactive:
   confirmed SeDenyInteractiveLogonRight and SeDenyRemoteInteractiveLogonRight both target
   DISTRICT\Domain Admins (SID S-1-5-21-2288391267-384259729-2991373820-512). District Lockdown
   had zero matches for those terms — not the cause.

3. Same GPOReport XML, grepped for SOMPath/LinksTo: single link, SOMPath "district.local"
   (domain root), Enabled true, NoOverride false. No dedicated OU, no DC exclusion.

4. Snapshot taken before any change:
   qm snapshot 100 pre-secure-admin-ws-scope-fix-20260901
   Verified registered via qm listsnapshot both before and after (host shell output above).

5. Deny-Apply ACE added for the "Domain Controllers" group on the Secure Admin WS GPO object,
   via the GroupPolicy AD: PSDrive and the well-known "Apply Group Policy" extended right GUID
   (edacfd8f-ffb3-11d1-b41d-00a0c968f939):

   Import-Module GroupPolicy,ActiveDirectory
   $gpo = Get-GPO -Name "Secure Admin WS"
   $gpoPath = "AD:\" + $gpo.Path
   $dcGroup = Get-ADGroup -Identity "Domain Controllers"
   $applyGPO = New-Object Guid "edacfd8f-ffb3-11d1-b41d-00a0c968f939"
   $acl = Get-Acl -Path $gpoPath
   $identity = New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier($dcGroup.SID)
   $rule = New-Object System.DirectoryServices.ActiveDirectoryAccessRule($identity, "ExtendedRight", "Deny", $applyGPO)
   $acl.AddAccessRule($rule)
   Set-Acl -Path $gpoPath -AclObject $acl

   Result: { "exitcode": 0, "exited": 1, "out-data": "ACE added\r\n" }

6. Verification: gpupdate /force /target:computer; gpresult /r /scope:computer

   Result: { "exitcode": 0, "exited": 1, ... }
   "Applied Group Policy Objects": Default Domain Controllers Policy, DC - Secure LDAP,
     Default Domain Policy, District Lockdown, Local Group Policy
   "The following GPOs were not applied because they were filtered out":
     Secure Admin WS — Filtering: Denied (Security)

Conclusion: Secure Admin WS no longer applies to DC01. The Domain-Admins-tier logon deny rule
is confirmed gone from this machine's effective policy — captured via gpresult's own filtering
report, not inferred from the ACL change alone. Whether Administrator can actually log in now
is the next thing to test, separately, at the console.
